<!-- Provenance: captured with the SEMPv1 show-redundancy request from the
     Backup member of a real Active/Standby HA pair, after activity had moved
     to it and the group had returned to health (SOL-151996).

     This exists to decouple two properties that show_redundancy_released.xml
     conflates. That fixture has a populated <backup> subtree AND
     redundancy-status Down, so a test using it alone cannot tell whether a
     reader keys off the role or off group health. Here the pair is fully
     healthy — redundancy-status Up, both mate-link flags true — while the
     Backup is still the active member with the populated subtree. Anything
     that treats "activity on the backup role" as inherently degraded fails
     against this fixture and passes against the other.

     How activity comes to rest on the Backup: `release-activity` on the
     Primary, then `no release-activity` to clear it. With auto-revert false
     (the wire value below) the Primary does NOT take activity back when it
     returns — it rejoins as a healthy standby and the Backup stays active
     indefinitely. That is a normal steady state for such a pair, not a fault,
     and it is the state captured here.

     Distinguishing it from show_redundancy_released.xml on the wire:
     redundancy-status flips Down -> Up, and priority-reported-by-mate reports
     the returned mate as "Standby" / "Standby (150)" instead of "Release" /
     "Release (0)". Everything else is identical, including the populated
     backup subtree and the bare-<activity> primary subtree.

     Scrubbed capture: group-management-server-identity, mate-router-name, and
     the VRRP static-address are synthetic placeholders, not the real service's
     identity. Do not paste a raw re-capture over them. Values are kept
     consistent with show_redundancy_released.xml — same pair, same member, so
     the same placeholders. Conventions:
     test/performance/mock-semp/canned/sanitize.local.tsv.example -->
<rpc-reply semp-version="broker/10.25.24.8245">
  <rpc>
    <show>
      <redundancy>
        <config-status>Enabled</config-status>
        <redundancy-status>Up</redundancy-status>
        <operating-mode>Message Routing Node</operating-mode>
        <switchover-mechanism>Hostlist</switchover-mechanism>
        <auto-revert>false</auto-revert>
        <redundancy-mode>Active/Standby</redundancy-mode>
        <active-standby-role>Backup</active-standby-role>
        <group-management-server-identity>testha01.messaging.example.com</group-management-server-identity>
        <mate-router-name>testha01primary</mate-router-name>
        <oper-status>
          <adb-link-up>true</adb-link-up>
          <adb-hello-up>true</adb-hello-up>
        </oper-status>
        <failover-criteria>any-fail</failover-criteria>
        <vrrp-interfaces>
          <interface>
            <name>intf0</name>
            <static-address>198.51.100.11</static-address>
            <static-status>Up</static-status>
          </interface>
        </vrrp-interfaces>
        <virtual-routers>
          <primary>
            <config>
              <routing-interface>intf0:1</routing-interface>
              <vrrp-vrid>-1</vrrp-vrid>
            </config>
            <status>
              <activity>Shutdown</activity>
            </status>
          </primary>
          <backup>
            <config>
              <routing-interface>intf0:1</routing-interface>
              <vrrp-vrid>-1</vrrp-vrid>
            </config>
            <status>
              <activity>Local Active</activity>
              <vrrp>Initialize</vrrp>
              <vrrp-interfaces>
                <interface>
                  <name>intf0</name>
                  <status>Initialize</status>
                </interface>
              </vrrp-interfaces>
              <routing-interface>Up</routing-interface>
              <vrrp-priority>240</vrrp-priority>
              <priority-reported-by-mate>
                <summary>Standby</summary>
                <cspf>None</cspf>
                <adb-hello>Standby</adb-hello>
                <vrrp>Standby (150)</vrrp>
                <vrrp-interfaces>
                  <interface>
                    <name>intf0</name>
                    <priority>Standby (150)</priority>
                  </interface>
                </vrrp-interfaces>
              </priority-reported-by-mate>
            </status>
          </backup>
        </virtual-routers>
      </redundancy>
    </show>
  </rpc>
<execute-result code="ok"/>
</rpc-reply>
